{"ts":"2026-10-10T08:00:54.487Z","type":"agent_event","event":{"type":"content_end","contentType":"tool","toolName":"read_files","toolCallId":"toolu_01Gf8Ho6s68sXUcYT6TEhBMM","output":[{"query":"C:\\Dev\\cline hackathon\\build\\memory-bank\\projectbrief.md","result":" 1 | # Project brief: KaagazCheck (कागज़ चेक)\n 2 | \n 3 | **One-line pitch:** The government checks your papers after you apply and rejects you months later. KaagazCheck checks them *before* you apply, in Hindi, and tells you exactly what to fix.\n 4 | \n 5 | ## Problem (evidence in docs/research.md)\n 6 | Many eligible people lose welfare benefits *after* applying, because their documents don't agree with each other (name spellings across Aadhaar, bank and land record; Aadhaar not linked to the bank; missing or expired certificates) or because a rejection arrives with a reason they can't understand. Example: in Rajasthan in 2019, only about 23% of registered PM-KISAN farmers received the third instalment, mostly because of name mismatches with Aadhaar.\n 7 | \n 8 | ## Users\n 9 | - **Primary:** a low-income citizen (farmer, widow, woman of an Assam household) on a basic Android phone, often helped by a family member or a CSC/NGO helper.\n10 | - **Admin:** a helper organisation that adds new schemes (uses the Cline agent).\n11 | \n12 | ## Scope: 3 schemes, Hindi + English\n13 | PM-KISAN · Orunodoi 3.0 (Assam) · Widow Pension (IGNWPS, NSAP). Rules and sources: `docs/schemes.md`. Every rule carries its `sourceUrl`.\n14 | \n15 | ## Core loop (must-ship demo path)\n16 | 1. Pick a scheme.\n17 | 2. Add documents: upload photos, or tap \"Use sample\" (3 fake sample sets: clean, name mismatch, missing document).\n18 | 3. AI reads each document → fields with a confidence level. Low confidence → user confirms or edits.\n19 | 4. Answer 3–5 yes/no exclusion questions (e.g. \"Did anyone in the family pay income tax last year?\").\n20 | 5. **Rule engine (pure code)** produces a readiness report: 🟢 ready / 🔴 fix first / ⚪ not eligible / 🟡 confirm at office, with each check, its reason, where to fix it (bank, Aadhaar centre, tehsil, Gram Panchayat) and its source.\n21 | 6. Report shown in Hindi (English toggle) + a printable cover sheet with pre-filled fields to carry to the office.\n22 | \n23 | ## Second mode: rejection decoder (T4)\n24 | Paste or photograph a rejection or status message → AI maps it to a known reason code (enum) → code-mapped fix steps + an AI-drafted correction or grievance letter in Hindi.\n25 | \n26 | ## Cline inside the product: Scheme Onboarding Agent (T3)\n27 | Admin pastes a new scheme's guideline text → a `@cline/sdk` Agent with tools (`getRuleSchema`, `proposeScheme`, `runSchemeTests`) writes a scheme rule definition (validated by zod) plus test cases (an eligible and an ineligible applicant), runs them through our rule engine, and saves a **pending** proposal. A human approves → the scheme goes live. Demo input: `docs/sample-guideline-ignoaps.md` (Old Age Pension).\n28 | \n29 | ## Trust rules (non-negotiable)\n30 | - The AI only reads and explains. **Eligibility and readiness are decided by the tested rule engine**, never by model text.\n31 | - Aadhaar numbers are masked to the last 4 digits everywhere (UI, logs, printouts). Uploaded images are processed in memory and not stored.\n32 | - Uncertain → say \"confirm at the office\", never guess.\n33 | - Demo uses fake sample documents only.\n34 | \n35 | ## Data model sketch\n36 | - `SchemeRule` { id, name, nameHi, sourceUrl, requiredDocs[], ageRange?, eligibility (any-of flags), exclusions (question ids), conflicts[], notes }\n37 | - `ExtractedDoc` { docType: aadhaar | bank_passbook | land_record | ration_card | death_certificate | income_certificate | other, fields { name, dob, aadhaarLast4, accountNo, ifsc, aadhaarLinked?, issueDate?, ... }, confidence per field }\n38 | - `Answers` { [questionId]: boolean }\n39 | - `ReadinessReport` { status, checks: [{ id, status: pass | fail | uncertain, messageEn, messageHi, fixWhere, source }] }\n40 | - DB (SQLite, T3 only): `scheme_proposals` { id, schemeJson, testsJson, testResult, status: pending | approved | rejected, createdAt }\n41 | \n42 | ## Stretch (only if ahead)\n43 | Read the report aloud in Hindi (browser `speechSynthesis`, hi-IN) · Assamese · helper mode for many applicants.\n44 | \n45 | ## Cut list (if behind, cut in this order)\n46 | Voice → rejection decoder letter drafting → real photo upload (keep sample sets + manual entry) → printable cover sheet styling.","success":true},{"query":"C:\\Dev\\cline hackathon\\build\\memory-bank\\README.md","result":"1 | # Memory Bank\n2 | Empty until the problem statement is released. In task T0, Cline creates:\n3 | `projectbrief.md` (from Claude's brief), `productContext.md`, `systemPatterns.md`, `techContext.md`, `activeContext.md`, `progress.md`.","success":true},{"query":"C:\\Dev\\cline hackathon\\build\\docs\\PS.md","result":" 1 | # Cline AI Builder Hackathon: Round 2 Problem Statement\n 2 | \n 3 | India has problems that millions of people live with every day. What would you build with AI to solve one of them?\n 4 | \n 5 | India is building at a scale few countries have ever attempted, yet for hundreds of millions of people daily life still runs into the same walls. A farmer cannot tell what is wrong with his crop until the damage is done. A patient from a small town travels for hours to meet a doctor who has only a few minutes to give her. A student studies from notes written in a language she does not think in. A citizen loses months to a government scheme he qualified for but never heard about.\n 6 | \n 7 | These are not rare cases. They repeat across healthcare, education, agriculture, governance, finance, safety and climate, and across languages, incomes and literacy levels. AI can finally make a real dent: it understands Indian languages and voice, reads messy documents, reasons over local data and can reach anyone with a basic smartphone.\n 8 | \n 9 | **The challenge:** pick one real problem that Indian society faces and, in 8 hours, build an AI-powered solution for it, using Cline as your AI coding partner from the first commit to the last. You can also build Cline into the solution itself, for example with the Cline SDK. Not a slide deck, but a working product that a real person could use tomorrow.\n10 | \n11 | The problem is yours to choose. Healthcare, education, agriculture, governance, finance, safety and climate are only some examples, so look at the problems around you and pick one that matters. For example, potholes are a problem on roads across India, and one solution is a dashcam in a car with AI that spots potholes as it drives and prepares the complaint automatically.\n12 | \n13 | ## Deliverables\n14 | - **Codebase on GitHub (with README):** a public repository with a clear README covering the problem, your solution, architecture, tech stack, setup steps and how Cline was used in the build.\n15 | - **PPT:** a presentation covering the problem, your solution, the architecture, the AI models and tools involved, how Cline was used (in the build and inside the solution), challenges faced and future scope.\n16 | - **Demo video (mandatory):** a short walkthrough of your working solution with a real user scenario, showing Cline in action during the build.\n17 | - **Social media post (brownie points):** share your build, demo, experience or participation shoutout, and tag Cline.\n18 | \n19 | ## Evaluation criteria\n20 | Creativity & uniqueness · Innovation · Real-world usefulness · Effective use of Cline · Product usability & overall execution · Technical implementation. **Bonus** for solutions that use Cline inside the product itself, not only to build it.","success":true},{"query":"C:\\Dev\\cline hackathon\\build\\docs\\schemes.md","result":" 1 | # The three schemes: rules for the rule engine\n 2 | \n 3 | Use `sourceUrl` exactly as given. Where a rule is unclear, the engine returns `uncertain` (\"confirm at the office\"), never a guess.\n 4 | \n 5 | ## 1. PM-KISAN (id: `pm-kisan`)\n 6 | sourceUrl: https://pmkisan.gov.in/ (summary: https://en.vikaspedia.in/viewcontent/agriculture/policies-and-schemes/crops-related/comprehensive-guide-to-the-pradhan-mantri-kisan-samman-nidhi-(pm-kisan)-scheme?lgn=en)\n 7 | - Benefit: ₹6,000/year, 3 instalments of ₹2,000, by DBT.\n 8 | - Eligible: a farmer family (husband, wife, minor children) owning cultivable land per state land records.\n 9 | - Exclusion questions (any \"yes\" → not eligible):\n10 |   - `holds_constitutional_post`: current/former constitutional post, minister, MP, MLA, mayor, or district panchayat chairperson in the family?\n11 |   - `govt_employee`: serving or retired government/PSU officer or employee (not MTS, Class IV or Group D)?\n12 |   - `income_tax_payer`: anyone paid income tax in the last assessment year?\n13 |   - `practising_professional`: practising doctor, engineer, lawyer, CA or architect?\n14 |   - `pension_10k_plus`: pensioner getting ₹10,000+/month (not MTS, Class IV or Group D)?\n15 | - Required docs: `aadhaar`, `bank_passbook`, `land_record`.\n16 | - Document checks: name matches across aadhaar, bank_passbook and land_record; bank shows Aadhaar linked (if the field is missing → uncertain, with a fix: \"get Aadhaar seeded at your bank branch\"); IFSC format valid (`^[A-Z]{4}0[A-Z0-9]{6}$`).\n17 | - Fix map: name mismatch → correct at the Aadhaar centre or the bank, or get the land record corrected at the tehsil/circle office; Aadhaar not linked → bank branch; eKYC → PM-KISAN portal OTP or CSC biometric.\n18 | \n19 | ## 2. Orunodoi 3.0, Assam (id: `orunodoi-3`)\n20 | sourceUrl: https://www.publicservicesmap.in/schemes/orunodoi-3.0--aos?lang=en (nodal: Finance Department, Government of Assam)\n21 | - Benefit: ₹1,250/month by DBT to one nominated woman of the household.\n22 | - Precondition: household has an NFSA ration card (`ration_card` required).\n23 | - Eligible if ANY one is true (questions): `widow_or_unmarried_45plus`, `transgender`, `pwd`, `old_or_infirm_woman`, `earners_disabled_or_60plus`, `member_serious_illness` (HIV, thalassemia, haemophilia, cerebral palsy, leprosy, autism), `homeless`, `destitute`, `aay_beneficiary`, `household_income_under_2l`.\n24 | - Exclusion questions: `elected_representative` (current/former MP, MLA, CEM, EM; current PRI/ULB/council member), `professional_or_teacher` (doctor, engineer, architect, CA, bank official, lawyer, any teacher), `govt_employee_or_pensioner` (incl. contractual, Anganwadi, ASHA, mid-day meal), `contractor_or_taxpaying_trader`, `large_landholder` (more than small/marginal), `owns_four_wheeler`, `gets_similar_state_cash_scheme`.\n25 | - Conflict: if the person also gets the NSAP Widow Pension, whether that counts as a \"similar state cash scheme\" is unclear → `uncertain`, \"confirm at the Gram Panchayat\".\n26 | - Required docs: `aadhaar`, `bank_passbook`, `ration_card`. Also listed: domicile, voter ID, age proof, address proof, self-declaration (show as a checklist, not machine-checked).\n27 | - Document checks: the woman's name matches across aadhaar, bank_passbook and ration_card.\n28 | - Process: offline form → Gram Panchayat/committee → acknowledgement slip.\n29 | \n30 | ## 3. Widow Pension, IGNWPS under NSAP (id: `nsap-widow`)\n31 | sourceUrl: https://en.vikaspedia.in/viewcontent/social-welfare/social-security/pension-benefits-under-nsap?lgn=en\n32 | - Eligible: widow, age 40–59 inclusive (from Aadhaar DOB), BPL household (question `bpl_household`).\n33 | - Benefit: central share ₹200/month; the state adds at least an equal amount. Don't quote a state total.\n34 | - Required docs: `aadhaar`, `death_certificate` (of the husband), `bank_passbook`; BPL proof (checklist).\n35 | - Document checks: the applicant's name matches across aadhaar and bank_passbook; the husband's name on death_certificate is present.\n36 | - Age outside 40–59 → not eligible for this scheme (age 60+ → suggest Old Age Pension, once it is onboarded by the agent).\n37 | \n38 | ## Name matching rules (core showcase)\n39 | - Normalise: uppercase, strip punctuation, collapse spaces, drop honorifics (SHRI, SMT, MR, MRS, KUMARI, LATE).\n40 | - Expand common abbreviations: KR → KUMAR, MD/MOHD → MOHAMMAD, DEVI/DEBI treated as equal, SK → SHEIKH.\n41 | - Basic Devanagari → Latin transliteration for names written in Hindi.\n42 | - Initials: \"R. KUMAR\" can match \"RAMESH KUMAR\" only as `uncertain`, never `pass`.\n43 | - Scores: pass at ≥ 0.92 similarity (Jaro-Winkler on the normalised form), uncertain from 0.80 to under 0.92, fail below 0.80. Joined vs spaced (\"RAMESHKUMAR\" vs \"RAMESH KUMAR\") → pass.","success":true},{"query":"C:\\Dev\\cline hackathon\\build\\docs\\research.md","result":" 1 | # Research summary (for README and slides)\n 2 | \n 3 | ## Why not a \"scheme finder\"\n 4 | Saturated: the government's myScheme portal already finds schemes, and Microsoft + AI4Bharat launched Jugalbandi, a voice WhatsApp scheme bot in Indian languages, in 2023 (https://techcrunch.com/2023/05/24/microsoft-ai-chatgpt-reaches-rural-india/).\n 5 | \n 6 | ## The real gap: people lose benefits AFTER applying\n 7 | - **Rajasthan, PM-KISAN (2019):** only about 23% of about 51 lakh registered farmers received the third instalment; names on applications did not match Aadhaar. Under 1% were paid in Ganganagar district. Source: Hindustan Times, https://www.pressreader.com/india/hindustan-times-st-jaipur/20191007/281586652343870\n 8 | - **Andhra Pradesh, PM-KISAN (LibTech India, Jan 2022):** about 15.2 lakh farmers missed instalments, about ₹1,343 crore pending; 47% bank rejections, 51% \"action required by the state\" with no reason given; only 12.5% of 33,526 studied cases were resolved, some pending for 1,060+ days. Source: https://libtech.in/wp-content/uploads/2022/01/‘PM-KISAN-Samman-Nidhi-AP-25th-January.pdf\n 9 | - **Documents as the gate (Haqdarshak):** a cited study attributes 20% of beneficiary exclusion to documentation issues; 45% of surveyed drivers had trouble submitting forms; middlemen charged ₹150–300 for a card priced at ₹35. Advocacy source. https://yojanacard.haqdarshak.com/?p=9509\n10 | \n11 | Present these as documented examples (old and regional), not current national totals.\n12 | \n13 | ## Pitch\n14 | \"He qualified. He applied. He got nothing, because of one letter in his name. KaagazCheck catches that before he applies.\"","success":true},{"query":"C:\\Dev\\cline hackathon\\build\\docs\\README.md","result":"1 | # docs/\n2 | - `PS.md`: the Round 2 problem statement (owned by Claude and the user; Cline does not edit).\n3 | - `research.md`: evidence and pitch for README and slides.\n4 | - `schemes.md`: rules for the three schemes (source of truth for the rule engine).\n5 | - `sample-guideline-ignoaps.md`: demo input for the Scheme Onboarding Agent (T3).","success":true},{"query":"C:\\Dev\\cline hackathon\\build\\tasks\\LOG.md","result":"1 | # Build log (Cline appends after every task)","success":true},{"query":"C:\\Dev\\cline hackathon\\build\\tasks\\README.md","result":"1 | # tasks/\n2 | - `NEXT.md` — the one task Cline does next (written by Claude).\n3 | - `backlog/` — the day's tasks. T0 and T-last are ready; Claude writes T1–T4 after reading the problem statement.\n4 | - `LOG.md` — Cline's report after each task.","success":true},{"query":"C:\\Dev\\cline hackathon\\build\\tasks\\backlog\\T0-kickoff.md","result":" 1 | # T0 — Memory Bank + scaffold (first 30 minutes after the idea is chosen)\n 2 | \n 3 | Mode: PLAN first (show it to judges), then ACT.\n 4 | \n 5 | 1. Read `docs/PS*` and `memory-bank/projectbrief.md` (written by Claude).\n 6 | 2. Create the Memory Bank files: productContext, systemPatterns, techContext, activeContext, progress (short and factual).\n 7 | 3. `git init` if needed. Scaffold a Next.js App Router app (TypeScript strict, Tailwind) IN THIS FOLDER, keeping `memory-bank/`, `tasks/`, `docs/`, `.clinerules/`, `.cline-runs/`.\n 8 | 4. Add Vitest + fast-check; npm scripts `test`, `build`, `lint`, `typecheck`.\n 9 | 5. `.gitignore`: `.env.local`, `node_modules`, `.next`, `*.db` — NOT `.cline-runs/`.\n10 | 6. One smoke test; `npm test` and `npm run build` pass.\n11 | \n12 | **Done when:** tests and build pass, Memory Bank exists, first commit made, LOG.md updated.","success":true},{"query":"C:\\Dev\\cline hackathon\\build\\tasks\\backlog\\T1-core-engine.md","result":" 1 | # T1: Core rule engine, tests first (about 60–75 min)\n 2 | \n 3 | Mode: ACT. Pure TypeScript in `src/core/` only: no UI, network, AI or database imports. Read `docs/schemes.md` first.\n 4 | \n 5 | 1. `src/core/types.ts` + zod schemas: `SchemeRule`, `ExtractedDoc`, `Answers`, `ReadinessReport` (see memory-bank/projectbrief.md, \"Data model sketch\").\n 6 | 2. `src/core/schemes/` with `pm-kisan.ts`, `orunodoi-3.ts`, `nsap-widow.ts` as **data** validated by the `SchemeRule` schema, each with `sourceUrl`, required docs, eligibility/exclusion question ids with English + Hindi question text. Export a registry `getScheme(id)`, `listSchemes()`, plus `registerScheme(rule)` for schemes added later (T3).\n 7 | 3. `src/core/nameMatch.ts`: normalise, honorifics, abbreviations, basic Devanagari→Latin transliteration, Jaro-Winkler; returns `{ status: pass | uncertain | fail, score }` with the thresholds in docs/schemes.md.\n 8 | 4. `src/core/age.ts`: age on a given date from DOB (inject \"today\" for testability).\n 9 | 5. `src/core/aadhaar.ts`: `maskAadhaar()` → `XXXX XXXX 1234`; never return the full number.\n10 | 6. `src/core/evaluate.ts`: `evaluate(scheme, docs, answers, today) → ReadinessReport`. Checks: required docs present; name consistency across the scheme's docs; age window; eligibility any-of; exclusions; conflicts → uncertain; IFSC format; Aadhaar-bank link. Each check has `messageEn`, `messageHi`, `fixWhere`, `source`. Overall status precedence: not_eligible > fix_first > confirm_at_office > ready.\n11 | 7. `src/core/rejectionCodes.ts`: enum of known reasons (`NAME_MISMATCH_AADHAAR`, `AADHAAR_NOT_SEEDED_BANK`, `INVALID_ACCOUNT_IFSC`, `ACCOUNT_INACTIVE`, `LAND_NOT_VERIFIED`, `EKYC_PENDING`, `PENDING_STATE_ACTION`, `UNKNOWN`) → fix steps in English + Hindi.\n12 | \n13 | **Tests (Vitest, write them first):**\n14 | - nameMatch: \"Ramesh Kumar\" vs \"RAMESH KR.\" → pass; \"RAMESHKUMAR\" vs \"Ramesh Kumar\" → pass; \"Smt. Sunita Devi\" vs \"SUNITA DEBI\" → pass; \"R. Kumar\" vs \"Ramesh Kumar\" → uncertain; \"Ramesh Kumar\" vs \"Suresh Kumar\" → fail; \"रमेश कुमार\" vs \"Ramesh Kumar\" → pass.\n15 | - fast-check properties: a name always matches itself; the match is symmetric; `maskAadhaar` output never contains more than 4 digits.\n16 | - evaluate: clean PM-KISAN set → ready; name mismatch on the bank passbook → fix_first with fixWhere \"bank\"; missing land_record → fix_first; income tax yes → not_eligible; widow aged 39 → not_eligible; widow aged 59 → eligible; Orunodoi + gets NSAP widow pension → confirm_at_office.\n17 | \n18 | **Done when:** `npm test` passes with all of the above, `npm run typecheck` is clean, LOG.md updated, committed as \"T1: core rule engine\".","success":true},{"query":"C:\\Dev\\cline hackathon\\build\\.cline-runs\\index.md","result":"1 | - 2026-10-09T21:20:28.679Z | run-025028-mcen | plan | done (exit 0) | 10s | Connection test only. Reply with exactly: BRIDGE OK. Do not create, edit or delete any files and do ","success":true}],"durationMs":46}}
