{"ts":"2026-10-10T08:01:22.487Z","type":"agent_event","event":{"type":"content_end","contentType":"reasoning","reasoning":"I recall that create-next-app only refuses to scaffold if the target directory has conflicting files, but it allows a specific whitelist like .git, .gitignore, README.md, and similar metadata files to already exist.\n\nSince this project already has memory-bank, tasks, and config folders present, those would conflict, so I'm thinking through two approaches: scaffolding into a temp sibling directory and copying files over afterward, or manually writing package.json and installing Next.js directly for tighter control. I'm leaning toward the temp-scaffold approach since it preserves canonical config files like ESLint, PostCSS, and Tailwind v4 setup, and I need to settle on the right create-next-app flags to avoid interactive prompts while matching the project's existing src/core structure.\n\nI need to account for app/ living at root rather than under src/, so I'll use `--no-src-dir` with the import alias still mapping correctly. I'm also factoring in that Next.js 16 removed `next lint` in favor of running ESLint directly, defaults to Turbopack for builds, and I should wire up Vitest with a meaningful smoke test — exporting something like APP_NAME from a core module and testing that it's used by the page, rather than a throwaway trivial test.\n\nI'm also thinking through environment risks: the Windows path containing a space needs quoting in scripts, Node 24 satisfies Next 16's Node 20.9+ requirement, and I should use `--yes` and `--disable-git` flags with create-next-app to avoid interactive prompts and unwanted git initialization, since we're skipping the copied .git directory anyway.\n\nI'll plan to review and possibly exclude any auto-generated agent docs so they don't conflict with .clinerules, and replace the scaffolded README with a short placeholder stub rather than a full one. For Devanagari rendering, I'll need a font like Noto Sans Devanagari via next/font/google, which requires network access during build to fetch.\n\nGiven the build-time fetch risk, I'll drop Geist and rely on the system font stack (Nirmala UI, Noto) instead, and set the page's base lang to \"en\" with a \"hi\" span for the Hindi text. I also spot a critical issue: create-next-app's gitignore pattern `.env*` would accidentally ignore `.env.example`, so I need to fix that with a negation rule, and double-check `.cline-runs/` isn't ignored either.\n\nBefore committing, I should verify git identity is configured globally and confirm npm registry access is reachable, then append to LOG.md and update the memory bank.\n\nI've reviewed the task file, rules, and backlog. Before drafting the plan, I'll verify current package versions, git user identity, and npm registry reachability.\n\n"}}
